# TRX64 SDK @SDK_VERSION@

This package builds a library for the generic core application, the menu record of that library, the app of a core linked from its TOML A and TOML B, and a `.cor` bundle that carries them.
It needs nothing from the TRX64 repository.

The document [`api.md`](api.md) is the contract of the interface a library and an application use.

The file `VERSION` names the SDK version, the base API version (@ABI_MAJOR@.@ABI_MINOR@), the source commit and the SHA-256 of `lib/libtrxbase.a`.
It also names the SHA-256 of the public headers (`api_headers_sha256`, see "The version of the base API").

## Contents

| Folder | Content |
|---|---|
| `api.md` | The API of the core application: the entry points, the call tables, the operation types and the record of a library. Read it before the headers. |
| `include/` | The headers a library or an application includes: `libapi.h` and everything it includes (`svctab.h`, `ops.h`, `fsif.h`, the call tables of base 3.3 `svc_sync.h`, `svc_settings.h`, `svc_ctx.h`, `svc_io.h`, `svc_board.h`, `svc_overlay.h`, `svc_fs.h`, `svc_sys.h`; of base 3.4: `svc_wb.h`), `libfmt.h`, `glue.h`, `u2p_app.h`, `u2p_ui.h`, `hidden.h`, `textgrid.h`, `core_hooks.h` and the export tables a library finds with `api->lib->find` (`c64_api.h`, `c64cart_api.h`, `cbm1541_api.h`, `cbmprg_api.h`, `sid_api.h`, `tempdisk_api.h`, `mouse_api.h`, `pad_api.h`, `padkeys_api.h`). Their hash, comments and white space taken out, is `api_headers_sha256` in `VERSION`. |
| `mk/` | `lib.mk` (a library), `app.mk` (an application of C files, or a library with `APP_KIND = lib`), `lib.ld` (the linker script), `sdkroot.mk` (finds the package), `appflags.mk` and `appbuild.mk` (the app of a core, run by `tools/app_build.py build`). Both build with tail calls off, so a call into the base carries the return address of the image that makes it (the account of an allocation, a socket or a job). |
| `sdk/` | The C sources that `app.mk` links into an application. `lib.mk` links `sdk/libc.c` when `LIB_LIBC = 1`. |
| `test/` | `drv_fake.h`, the register fake of a native test (`api.md` 6.17.7): built with `-DDRV_PTR_HEADER='"drv_fake.h"'`, every register access of the generated driver table lands in one array the test defines. |
| `tools/` | `u2a_pack.py` (image to `.u2a`), `descrec.py` (menu TOML to menu record), `cor_pack.py` (`.cor` bundle), `rtl_check.py` (TOML A against the RTL of your design, and the lint for literal I/O addresses in C/C++), `app_build.py` (the app of a core: its sizes, tables, link) and `libtab.py` (the library table of a TOML B). |
| `lib/`, `base/` | The base of the core application as archives and sources, and the shared libraries as sources. See "The app of a core". There is no app image. |
| `libs/`, `gateware/` | `shared.toml` of each: the TOML B (the catalog of the shared libraries) and the TOML A of the shared app, the references a core's own start from. |
| `menusim/`, `bin/menusim` | The menu simulator and the tree it builds from. See "The menu simulator". |
| `licenses/` | The licence texts of the third-party code the base links. `NOTICE` names it. |
| `handover/uci/` | Gideon's UCI server core (`command_intf.cc`, `network_target.cc` and their headers), handed to TRX64-Ultimate once with its `PROVENANCE` and licence (SDK 0.4.6). Nothing in the SDK links it; the base has no UCI code. |
| `example/` | A library of two source files with a menu TOML and a host test, and the example core's own TOML A (`example/gateware/`), TOML B and description (`example/app/`). |

## Requirements

- A RISC-V GCC for 32 bit with the newlib headers, for example `riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc` as in the TRX64 build image, GNU make and Python 3.
  The public headers (`fsif.h`, reached through `ops.h` and `libapi.h`) include `<stdlib.h>`, `<string.h>` and `<strings.h>`. A compiler without
  a C library, such as the Homebrew `riscv64-elf-gcc` (rv32 multilib, no newlib headers), stops at `stdlib.h: No such file or directory`.
- A host C++ compiler, for the example's test only.
- Pillow (`pip install pillow`), only when `cor_pack.py --icon` gets a `.png`. The other tools use Python's own modules.

Set `RV_PREFIX` to the compiler's prefix, for example `RV_PREFIX=riscv32-unknown-elf-`.

## Build a library

1. Write a `Makefile` that names the library and includes `mk/lib.mk`:

   ```make
   LIB_NAME = mylib
   LIB_VERSION = 1
   LIB_NEEDS = base>=3.0
   LIB_SRCS = mylib.cc helper.cc
   LIB_MENU = menu.toml
   include <path to the package>/mk/lib.mk
   ```

2. Run `make RV_PREFIX=riscv32-unknown-elf-`. The result is `mylib.u2a`.

`lib.mk` compiles every file of `LIB_SRCS` (`.cc` and `.c`) and links them into one image. The image is position independent: the loader
relocates it. The entry point is the function `lib_register`, which fills a `LibRecord` with the library's operations and state values.
`LIB_NEEDS` names the base API version the library needs. `LIB_INC = -Idir` adds include paths. `LIB_LIBC = 1` links `memcpy`, `memset` and
integer division from `sdk/libc.c`; the code runs without a C library and without a hardware divider.

## Declare a budget

A library or an application may declare what it takes. Put `LIB_BUDGET` (`APP_BUDGET` for `app.mk`) in the Makefile, a list of `key=number` words:

```make
LIB_BUDGET = image=8192 heap_keep=2048 heap_peak=4096 ops=3 states=1 settings=2 tabs=1
```

The keys are `image` (code, data and bss, bytes), `heap_keep` (the bytes the image holds through `api->mem` when it is idle: the loader refuses it when the image and this figure do not fit the room of the heap), `heap_peak` (the most it holds at once: a ceiling), `ops`, `states`, `events`, `nodes`, `file_actions`, `settings`,
`tabs` and `polls` (what `lib_register` and the menu record bring). A key left out has no figure of its own, and the limit is the platform's: 1 MiB for the image,
the heap of the core, the room left in the tables. A file without a budget loads as before.

The packer refuses an image over its own `image` figure. The loader refuses a library that is over any figure, or over the room left in a table, with a sentence that
names the library, the figure and the limit, for example `Library "mylib" is refused: it brings 7 operations, over its declared budget of 5.` Nothing of it stays: no
registration, no memory. A file with a budget needs base 3.2; the packer adds `base>=3.2` to its dependencies. Every block a library takes from `api->mem`, also in its
operations later, is on its own account, and unloading the library gives all of it back. `api.md` section 8 has the contract.

## Constructors, exports and the C64 table

The loader runs no constructor and no destructor (D19). The packer refuses an image that holds one (a non-empty `.init_array`, `.fini_array`, `.ctors` or `.dtors`, or `__cxa_atexit`,
`atexit`) and names the section or symbol, so a global object with a constructor does not build into a library: set the state up in `lib_register`.

A library offers call tables under names (`LibRecord.export_list`, `LIB_EXPORTS = ring=1 trx=1` in `lib.mk`), and what needs one asks `api->lib->find("ring", U2A3_VERSION(1, 0), &size)` or lists
it in `LIB_NEEDS` (`ring>=1`), whatever the providing library is called; a folder of libraries is loaded with the provider in front. `include/c64_api.h` declares the table `c64` (major 1) that the
cores of the C64 family provide. `api->trx`, `api->trx->ring` and `api->c64` leave the base API: new code uses `api->lib->need` (a `LIB_NEEDS` entry) where it lists the provider, and `api->lib->find` for a name that is looked up at run time. The base also answers `find` for the tables of the shared libraries it keeps (`c64cart`, `cbm1541`, `sid`, `tempdisk`; `uci` is not among them, `api.md` 9.5.5), and for the text grid that a `terminal` node names with its `grid` parameter (`textgrid`): `api.md` sections 9.5 and 9.6 have them. `api.md` section 9 has the contract. An image that lists a provider in `LIB_NEEDS` gets its table without a name lookup: `api->lib->need(n, &size)` returns the table of entry `n` of the list, numbered from 0 in the order of `LIB_NEEDS` (`api.md` 4.10.3).

## Core hooks and the settings of the base

A library that is the code of a core exports the table `CoreHooks` (`include/core_hooks.h`) as `corehooks`, major 1 (`LIB_EXPORTS = corehooks=1`). Besides the hooks of the core description it has `start`
(once, before the first poll), `menu_closed` (when the menu closes) and `base_setting_changed` (after a setting of the base changes, and once per such setting after `start`). A member that is null
is a call the base makes itself. The library that is the whole code of a core is named in the core's API TOML (`hooks = "<library name>"` in `[core]`; `cor_pack` refuses a bundle without it, or a file that does not list `corehooks=1`): the base then calls `init`, `release`, `warm_reset`, `cold_start`, `media_flush` and `media_pending` where it calls the members of the core description, and serves the `[mount]` slots through `slot_spec`, `slot_mount` and `slot_eject` (`api.md` 9.7.9 to 9.7.11). A library that exports `corehooks` is never unloaded to make room. The base owns six settings (`Joystick Input` and the five USB mouse settings): a library reads them by key and must not declare them; a descriptor that does is refused. `api.md` sections 4.13.19 and 9.5 have the contract.

## The version of the base API

This package is base API @ABI_MAJOR@.@ABI_MINOR@. The same number is `U2A_ABI_MAJOR` and `U2A_BASE_MINOR` in `include/libfmt.h`, the `base_api` line of
`base/version.toml` (the base of this package), and the `base_api` line of `VERSION`. A library or an application says in `LIB_NEEDS`
(`base>=@ABI_MAJOR@.@ABI_MINOR@`) which base API it needs; the loader refuses a file that needs a newer base, with a message that names both versions
("needs base 3.3, this base is 3.2"), and a file built for another major.

The public headers of this package are the files of `include/`. Their content hash, comments and blanks left out, starts with `@API_HASH@`.
In the source repository a lock file holds that hash for every version, and the lint is red when the headers of a released version change. There is one base minor per SDK release: what is added before the release stays under the coming minor (SDK 0.2.0 is base 3.2).

- **Minor** (`@ABI_MAJOR@.x`): an addition that a library built before it can ignore. A new call table at the end of `LibApi`, a new function at the
  end of a call table (every table carries its size), a new value of an enum.
- **Major** (`x.0`): anything else. A field that moves, changes size or goes, a changed meaning, a change of `LibRecord` or of a `Lib*Decl`
  (the loader compares the size of the record), a change of the `.u2a` format.

A library that declares a poll (`LibRecord::poll`, or `[poll]` in its menu record, not both: an operation without arguments and a period in milliseconds) is
polled by the core application from the moment it has registered until it is unloaded or, for an application, released (base 3.2). The poll never runs while the
menu has stopped the machine. A poll the core application cannot serve (no such operation, an operation that takes arguments, a period of 0, no free slot)
refuses a library that loads after the core has started. A library that loads before the start stays loaded: its poll is dropped and the log says why. A library
with a poll, menu nodes, file actions, settings or tabs is never unloaded to make room for an application; the application is refused instead.

## Build a menu record

The menu of a library is a TOML file. `descrec.py` lists every key.

- `LIB_MENU = menu.toml` in the Makefile puts the record into the `.u2a`.
- `python3 tools/descrec.py compile menu.toml menu.bin` makes the record alone. `python3 tools/descrec.py dump menu.bin` reads it back.

## Build a `.cor`

A `.cor` is one container: the core's bitstream, the application image, the menu record and the libraries. `cor_pack.py` writes two kinds.

A core that does not fit the host's partition is one full bitstream (a large or monolithic core):

```
python3 tools/cor_pack.py pack-large -o mycore.cor --id mycore --name "My Core" --version 1.0.0 \
    --bit core.bit --app app.bin --desc menu.toml --sizes toml_b.toml --lib mylib.u2a
```

A core that runs in the host's partition is a partial bitstream routed against one host:

```
python3 tools/cor_pack.py pack -o mycore.cor --name "My Core" --version 1.0.0 \
    --partial core.bit --app app.bin --host-key <host key> --sizes toml_b.toml \
    --desc menu.toml --lib mylib.u2a
```

This package does not make that partial bitstream or the host key: both come from routing the core against the host's checkpoint and floorplan, which
are in the TRX64U-MultiCore repository and not in this package. `python3 tools/cor_pack.py hostkey host.bit` prints the key of a host bitstream.
Until the host's routing inputs are published, a partial core cannot be built from the package alone; `pack` is here for the cores built in that repository.

Both commands take `--heap <bytes>` (the heap of the core application for this core), `--icon`, `--short` and `--files`.
`--ops <list>` checks every operation name and binding of `--desc` against a list of what the application exports; the tool that writes that list
is not in this package yet (see below). `python3 tools/cor_pack.py check mycore.cor` checks the bundle. For the other options read the head of
`tools/cor_pack.py`.

## The app of a core

There is no app image in this package. A core's app is linked from the package with the core's TOML A (its gateware, one block per register range) and its TOML B (what the
app is linked with and how large its maxima are), and the library table and the driver table are made when it is linked: no library is loaded at run time and no name is looked
up (T239.3 D31).

```
python3 tools/app_build.py build --sdk . --toml-a mycore/toml_a.toml --maps mycore --toml-b mycore/toml_b.toml --out out --src mycore --record out/desc.bin
```

`--maps` adds the register maps of the core's own blocks (`<name>.map.toml`) to the package's `maps/`. A core that is described by its record names no `desc` symbol: the core table of its app is empty and the start makes the descriptor from the record of the `.cor`; the code of that core is the library its record names (`hooks = "<library>"`, api.md 9.7). `example/` is such a core: its own TOML A (`example/gateware/`), TOML B and description (`example/app/`) and the library `example.u2a` that is its code.

The TOML B has `[app]` (`desc`: the symbol of the descriptor of a core that is compiled into the app, none for a core described by its record, `base_sources`: files of the package's `coreapp/cores` that hold it, `sources`: the core's own files, found in the
`--src` directories), the libraries (`[[library]]`, as `libs/shared.toml`: sources found in the package's `base/src` or in `--src`) and `[sizes]`. The record is compiled against the same
`[sizes]` (`descrec.py compile ... --sizes toml_b.toml`, `cor_pack.py pack ... --sizes toml_b.toml`), so the compiler and the app refuse the same record.

A core whose TOML A holds only its own blocks (the gateware window the host hands to layer 2) names, in `[app]`, `toml_a` (its TOML A, a file beside the TOML B: `--toml-a` may then be left out) and
`base_blocks` (the blocks of the package's `gateware/shared.toml`, or of `--base-toml-a`, that the shared drivers of the base name: the host's mode register, the drive window, the ICAP
loader). The app is linked against the core's blocks and those, and no other (`tools/descrec.py` `merge_toml_a`): the driver table, the linker fragment and `out/blocks.txt` hold them. A core of
this repository's tree builds its app so with `tools/sdk_core_app.sh <package> <core id> <out dir>`, which also checks the link map (`tools/libtab.py mapcheck`): the sources of the libraries
the TOML B names are in it, those of no other library and of no other core's code are.

`[sizes]` names the maxima of the registry (`ops`, `states`, `events`), of the contributions the libraries make to the menu (`contrib_*`), of the bug report (`report_bytes`) and of the
menu record (`record_bytes`, `rec_*`). A key that is left out keeps the default of the headers; `python3 tools/app_build.py table` lists them. A maximum is part of the layout
of every object that includes `ops.h`, `descrec.h`, `contrib.h` or `corereport.h`, so the package carries the sources of those objects (`base/`, listed in `base/SIZED.txt`) and compiles them
for the core; the objects of every other source are in `lib/libtrxbase.a`, with `liblwip.a`, `liblvgl.a` and the start-up objects.

The example core is `example/app/`: `make app` in `example/` links it. The TOML A is `gateware/shared.toml`, the reference.

## The menu simulator

`bin/menusim --desc example/app/desc.toml` opens the menu of a core on this machine, in a window, from its description: the identity (`[core]`), the tabs, the nodes, the settings and
their places. Change the TOML and press Cmd+R (Ctrl+R elsewhere): the menu is built again and the page stays. The place of every row (tab, group, order) is in the TOML, the base's own
rows included (`menusim/coreapp/base_ui.toml`). `--keys F3,DOWN,RETURN --shot out.png` runs headless and writes a picture. It needs a C++ compiler, python3 and SDL2 (`brew install sdl2`); the
first start compiles the application's sources natively (a few minutes), the next start only what changed. The core's own code is not in the simulator: a hook does nothing, and an
operation shows its row without running. The real app is checked in the emulator.

## The example

```
cd example
make RV_PREFIX=riscv32-unknown-elf-
make menu
make test
```

`make` builds `example.u2a` from `example.cc` and `greet.cc`, with `menu.toml` as its menu record. `make menu` writes `example.menu.bin`. `make app` links the example core's app (see above).
`make test` builds `example.cc` and `greet.cc` with the host compiler, registers the library against a fake `LibApi` and calls its operation.

## Not in this package yet

- The host's routing inputs (checkpoint, floorplan), which a partial core needs to get its bitstream and host key.
- The tool that writes the list of a core's operations, the input of `--ops` of `cor_pack.py`.
- A test rig for descriptor hooks and operations against a model of a core.

## What is new in 0.5.0 (base API 3.5, T262)

This release lets a library build lists, choices and tabs at run time with the menu's own tools, and removes three defects of the menu. A library built against 0.4.x loads and runs
unchanged: everything below is a new member at the end of a table, a new value of an enum, or a new key of a text (`api.md` 1.2.9).

- **Rows at run time (P1).** A node of kind `list` shows the rows of a state, one line each (value, label, right column, tone, up to three lines the row says about itself), and calls an
  operation with the index of the row the person takes; a last row that starts with `+` asks the library for more by the operation named in the node's `more`. No node is declared for a row.
  `api.md` 6.19 and `LN_LIST` (6.5).
- **Choices (P2).** `api->ctx->ask_list` (4.14.14) and the node key `pick = "@state"` (6.7.3, 6.19.3) open the base's list of choices for a library. The list has no fixed number of rows
  (it was 40): it is sized by its text, bounded by the memory budget.
- **Tabs that come and go (P3).** `show = "@state"` on a tab of a menu record: the tab is on the strip while the state has a value (6.11.7).
- **A busy caption for a job (P4).** The node key `busy` names the words a node's job shows, and a percentage shows only once the operation reports one (6.7.3). This also changes the job nodes of libraries built against 0.4.x: a job that never reports progress shows its words alone, no longer `0%` with an empty bar.
- **A source of the F3 search (P5).** A node of kind `list` at `F3/search` with `search`, `find` and `fields` makes a library a source: the search of the Library gets one form with a Source
  row, the fields of the source, and the results open as a list (6.20).
- **The key legend (P6).** `legend = "Label"` on a row of the key table puts the key into the foot legend of its tab (6.18.3).
- **Defects (P7, P8, P9).** A Run that waited for its fetch closes the menu. When the focused row of a screen hides, the focus goes to the next row that takes it. A value that changed on a
  screen is written into its row in place, so a RETURN on an operation row no longer builds the pane again (about 365,000 pixels became about 50,000 per RETURN) and no key is lost meanwhile.

## What is new in 0.5.1 (base API 3.5, T262 D4)

One round of fixes for the defects TRX64-Ultimate found in 0.5.0. A library built against 3.4 or 3.5 loads as before; where a fix changes what a screen shows, the line says so.

- **hw.cc without `cart_regs`.** An app whose TOML A has no block `cart_regs` compiles: no cartridge-port registers, `stop` and `mode_reset` do nothing (`api.md` 6.17.5.9).
- **A list node's count and a greyed row.** The count of a `list` node leaves out the `+` row and the `!` notes; the tone `disabled` greys a whole list row (`api.md` 6.19).
- **`ask` per use.** `ask_max` (1 to 255, default 24) and `ask_any` (every printable character) make `ask` a free text; without them it is the name of a file as before. A text prompt that an operation asks for (`ops_ask_text`) keeps the file-name rule as before and takes every printable character only when it asks with `any`.
- **Key rows in a library's menu record (`api.md` 6.18.5).** A library's `--menu` TOML may carry `[[key]]` rows (key or codes, op, label `legend`, `when`, as a core's `menu.toml`). While the library is loaded the menu merges them behind the core's rows; windows stay a core's.
- **`browse_at` and `browse_sheet` (`api->ctx`, `api.md` 4.14.15, 4.14.16).** One opens Browse on the folder of a path with the cursor on its file (not on `..`), the other also opens the file's action sheet, as RETURN does. Base 3.6; a library checks `SVC_HAS(api->ctx, browse_at)`.
- **A file whose size is unknown (`SVCFS_SIZE_UNKNOWN`, `api.md` 4.4.24.6).** A file system lists such a file with the size `SVCFS_SIZE_UNKNOWN`; the file list shows `-` instead of `0 KB`. A size that is known shows as before.
- **A form keeps no empty space.** When rows hide under a window that had scrolled, the window moves up until it is full, also while the selection stands on a button outside the list.
- **`goto_screen` redraws.** A `goto_screen` called by an operation the menu runs (a row, a key, a list row) draws the new screen at once; the next key no longer acts on it unseen.
- **The search tab with a source.** RETURN on the idle Search tab asks for a name as it does without a source; the form is `/`. The idle tab shows the text the form holds, and its side panel no longer says "No media found." (`api.md` 6.20.2).
- **`<-` from search results.** It goes back to the search form with the results kept; a row `Results` of the form opens them again (`api.md` 6.20.2).
- **A job keeps its form.** A node's job that started on a screen leaves the screen standing: the job's row carries the words (`busy`) and the percentage, the other rows stay. On a page of one row the words are the row's, the title is the tab's when it would repeat them (`api.md` 6.7.3).
- **The list page keeps its rows.** A key on the page of a `list` node (a move, a RETURN, a status line, rows that changed) writes the rows, the foot and the side panel in place; the page is built again only when its shape changes (about 365,000 pixels became 7,000 to 110,000 per key). The idle Search tab of the F3 Library no longer reads the folder it does not show (it held the tab blank for as long as the folder took).

## Licence

GNU General Public License, version 3 or later: `LICENSE`. `lib/` and `base/` also link and carry FreeRTOS, lwIP, LVGL and FatFs under their own
licences: the texts are in `licenses/`, and `NOTICE` names them, the source commit and the origin of the interface headers.
